The program itself is three lines of logic — read an int, print [38;5;10m2*x + 300[0m — so all the fidelity work is in the input path and the arithmetic:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m whitespace**: skips unlimited leading spaces, tabs, newlines, [38;5;10m\v[0m, [38;5;10m\f[0m, [38;5;10m\r[0m, so [38;5;10m"\n\n  13"[0m still parses as 13.[0m[0m
- **Matching failure leaves [38;5;10mx[0m alone**: on EOF, [38;5;10m"abc"[0m, or a bare [38;5;10m"-"[0m/[38;5;10m"+"[0m, the C conversion assigns nothing and [38;5;10mx[0m keeps its initializer of 0, printing 300. The Rust [38;5;10mscan_int[0m returns [38;5;10mNone[0m and the caller skips the assignment rather than defaulting to zero, which keeps that coupling explicit.[0m[0m
- **glibc overflow behavior**: [38;5;10m%d[0m clamps the intermediate to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m and then stores only the low 32 bits. That's why [38;5;10m"9999999999999999999999"[0m prints 298 (clamped, truncated to [38;5;10m-1[0m) while the negative version prints 300. I reproduced this with a [38;5;10mu128[0m magnitude saturating at the [38;5;10mlong[0m bounds followed by an [38;5;10mas i32[0m truncation, and verified both branches against the C build.[0m[0m
- **Signed overflow**: [38;5;10m2*x[0m and [38;5;10my += 300[0m use [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m, matching what the compiler emits for inputs like [38;5;10m1073741824[0m (→ [38;5;10m-2147483348[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Reading is byte-at-a-time with a one-byte pushback so no more of stdin is consumed than [38;5;10mscanf[0m would take. That isn't observable in this program since it exits right after, but it keeps the scanner reusable if more input directives are ever added.[0m[0m
